// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · VALUE INTERPRETATION  (string -> key / string -> type)
//
// The projections hand us config VALUES as raw symbols. Nothing downstream can use
// a raw symbol: `"com.example.Y"` is a class only once it is resolved to a type
// hash, and `"${a.b}"` is a dependency only once `a.b` is located in some other
// file. This file is that interpretation step — the one the projection headers
// each promised ("interpretation ... is a resolution-layer step") and that was
// never written.
//
// THREE JOBS
//   1. UNQUOTE       the parser stores an annotation argument with its source
//                    quotes: `@Value("${a.b}")` arrives as the 16-char symbol
//                    """${a.b}""". Every rule below reads cfg_clean, never the raw.
//   2. PLACEHOLDERS  scan `${key}` / `${key:default}` out of any value, from ANY
//                    of the four mechanisms, and link key -> key across files.
//   3. NAME -> TYPE  resolve a config string that names a class to a type hash,
//                    by FQN (client or lib), by nested-name, or — for an
//                    annotation argument, which sits in a .java file and so has
//                    imports — by simple name through the normal type resolver.
//
// UNKNOWNS ARE DECLARED, NEVER DROPPED — same contract as ambiguous_unknown in
// call_chain.dl. config_unresolved(Mechanism, Site, Detail, Reason) is EXPORTED, so
// its Reason vocabulary is listed here once rather than left implicit across the
// five files that derive it:
//   unresolved_class            a slot whose schema says "a class goes here" named
//                               one we cannot find (XML class attr/element, an
//                               FQN-shaped annotation arg, a class-valued key)
//   undefined_key               a ${...} placeholder whose key is in no scanned
//                               config file. Legitimate at runtime (env var, -D
//                               flag, config server, an unscanned profile) — which
//                               is exactly why it is stated, not dropped: it is the
//                               boundary of what the config graph can see
//   unbound_key                 a key nothing in the code binds, so the "which
//                               methods does this key affect" question has no answer
//   dangling_bean_ref           <property ref="…"/> naming a bean that is not defined
//   unsatisfied_injection_point an injection point no bean satisfies (commonly a
//                               library-typed slot filled by autoconfiguration)
//   spel_expression             #{...} — can name anything; out of scope by construction
//   uninterpretable_argument    the parser's own UNKNOWN fallback for an annotation
//                               argument (concatenation, constant ref, complex expr)
//
// COST. Every scan is DEMAND-BOUNDED: cfg_raw holds only the strings a knob in
// knobs.dl actually selected, so the char-level range() scans never run over
// arbitrary config. Name->type joins bind the name first (hash lookup on
// qualifiedName), never enumerating types.
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. UNQUOTE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// cfg_raw(Raw): every string this layer will inspect. Populated from the four
// mechanisms below; the ONE entry point for the char-level machinery, so the
// scans stay bounded to strings a knob selected.
// ANNOTATION arguments are taken from BOTH sides. An annotation on a dependency's type
// is part of the application's own wiring: @ConditionalOnProperty names a key the
// consumer sets, @Qualifier names a bean the consumer injects. Scoping this line to
// "client" left every library argument out of cfg_raw, so cfg_clean never derived a row
// for it and ann_arg, which joins cfg_clean, silently dropped it. #851 made the ATTACH
// points provenance-aware, which is why the annotation was found on the right library
// type and its arguments were still empty: a condition with no key rather than no
// condition, which is the hardest shape of this to see.
cfg_raw(v) :- annotation_arg(_, _, _, v, _, _, _, _, _), v != "".
// The FILE-based mechanisms stay client-only, deliberately. A dependency ships its own
// .properties, .yml and XML, and those configure the dependency's tests and defaults, not
// the application: merging them into the key space would let a library's own test value
// decide a question about the consumer's deployment. The consumer's files are the only
// ones that decide.
cfg_raw(v) :- xml_attr("client", _, v, _, _), v != "".
cfg_raw(v) :- xml_elem("client", _, _, v, _, _, _), v != "".
cfg_raw(v) :- prop_segment("client", _, v, _, _, _, _), v != "".
cfg_raw(v) :- yaml_prop("client", _, v, _, _, _, _, _), v != "".
cfg_raw(v) :- yaml_segment("client", _, v, _, _, _, _), v != "".

// Peel matched leading/trailing double quotes, repeatedly: """x""" -> ""x"" -> "x" -> x.
cfg_strip(v, v) :- cfg_raw(v).
cfg_strip(v, inner) :- cfg_strip(v, s),
    strlen(s) >= 2,
    substr(s, 0, 1) = "\"",
    substr(s, strlen(s) - 1, 1) = "\"",
    inner = substr(s, 1, strlen(s) - 2).
cfg_quoted(s) :- cfg_strip(_, s), strlen(s) >= 2,
    substr(s, 0, 1) = "\"", substr(s, strlen(s) - 1, 1) = "\"".
// cfg_clean(Raw, Clean) — the fully-unquoted form. Exactly one per Raw.
cfg_clean(v, s) :- cfg_strip(v, s), !cfg_quoted(s).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. PLACEHOLDERS  —  ${key} / ${key:default}, anywhere in a value
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// COST GATE: only strings that actually contain "${" enter the char-level scan.
// Without it every config string in the repo would be walked character by
// character; with it the scan is bounded to the handful that hold a placeholder.
cfg_ph_candidate(s) :- cfg_clean(_, s), contains("${", s).
// Position of every "${" opener. range(0, n) yields 0..n-1, so this reads every
// 2-char window of the string.
cfg_ph_open(s, i) :- cfg_ph_candidate(s), strlen(s) >= 3,
    i = range(0, strlen(s) - 1),
    substr(s, i, 2) = "${".
// The text after an opener, and the FIRST "}" in it — a placeholder never nests.
cfg_ph_rest(s, r) :- cfg_ph_open(s, i), r = substr(s, i + 2, strlen(s) - i - 2).
cfg_ph_close(r, i) :- cfg_ph_rest(_, r), i = range(0, strlen(r)), substr(r, i, 1) = "}".
cfg_ph_close_min(r, m) :- cfg_ph_close(r, _), m = min i : cfg_ph_close(r, i).
// cfg_ph_body(String, Body) — the text between "${" and "}", still possibly "key:default".
cfg_ph_body(s, b) :- cfg_ph_rest(s, r), cfg_ph_close_min(r, m), b = substr(r, 0, m).

// Split the body on its FIRST ":" — Spring's default-value separator.
cfg_ph_colon(b, i) :- cfg_ph_body(_, b), i = range(0, strlen(b)), substr(b, i, 1) = ":".
cfg_ph_colon_min(b, m) :- cfg_ph_colon(b, _), m = min i : cfg_ph_colon(b, i).
cfg_ph_has_default(b) :- cfg_ph_colon_min(b, _).
// cfg_placeholder(String, Key, Default) — the interpreted placeholder.
cfg_placeholder(s, b, "") :- cfg_ph_body(s, b), !cfg_ph_has_default(b).
cfg_placeholder(s, k, d) :- cfg_ph_body(s, b), cfg_ph_colon_min(b, m),
    k = substr(b, 0, m),
    d = substr(b, m + 1, strlen(b) - m - 1).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. THE UNIFIED KEY SPACE  —  .properties and .yaml behind one relation
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Spring merges application.properties and application.yml into ONE key space, so
// a placeholder written in YAML may resolve against a key defined in .properties.
// Modelling them separately would miss exactly that. Both parsers already emit
// fully-dotted keys (yaml `svc: {handler: x}` -> key "svc.handler"), so the merge
// is a union, not a re-derivation.
//
// config_key(Format, Key, File, KeyHash)
config_key("properties", k, f, h) :- prop_key("client", k, "true", f, h).
config_key("yaml", k, f, h) :- yaml_prop("client", k, v, vt, _, _, f, h),
    vt != "MAP", vt != "SEQUENCE", v != "".
config_key_defined(k) :- config_key(_, k, _, _).

// config_key_value(Format, Key, Value) — the value as written (placeholders intact).
// .properties has no value column; a single-segment value IS the value, and a
// multi-segment value is reassembled only far enough to carry its placeholders
// (cfg_placeholder already ran on each segment).
cfg_prop_seg_count(h, n) :- prop_key("client", _, _, _, h),
    n = count : { prop_segment("client", h, _, _, _, _, _) }.
config_key_value("properties", k, v) :- config_key("properties", k, _, h),
    cfg_prop_seg_count(h, 1),
    prop_segment("client", h, raw, "LITERAL", _, _, _),
    cfg_clean(raw, v).
config_key_value("yaml", k, v) :- config_key("yaml", k, _, h),
    yaml_prop("client", k, raw, _, _, _, _, h), cfg_clean(raw, v).

// ── key -> key: the PLACEHOLDER CHAIN ────────────────────────────────────────
// config_key_ref(Key, RefKey, Default): Key's value contains ${RefKey[:Default]}.
// Reads the SEGMENTS the parser already split (java_*_value_segment exists for
// exactly this) AND re-scans the raw value, because the two parsers disagree on
// the segment type for a YAML placeholder (properties says PROPERTY_REFERENCE,
// yaml says ENV_VARIABLE for the same `${a.b}`) — trusting either one alone
// silently loses one format. The re-scan is the ground truth; the segments supply
// the default value.
config_key_ref(k, ref, d) :- config_key("properties", k, _, h),
    prop_segment("client", h, raw, st, d, _, _),
    cfg_ref_segment_type(st),
    cfg_clean(raw, ref).
config_key_ref(k, ref, d) :- config_key("yaml", k, _, h),
    yaml_segment("client", h, raw, st, d, _, _),
    cfg_ref_segment_type(st),
    cfg_clean(raw, ref).
config_key_ref(k, ref, d) :- config_key_value(_, k, v), cfg_placeholder(v, ref, d).
// Segment types that mean "this is a reference to another key / the environment".
// Both parsers' vocabularies, in one place — see the disagreement noted above.
cfg_ref_segment_type("PROPERTY_REFERENCE").
cfg_ref_segment_type("PROPERTY_REF_WITH_DEFAULT").
cfg_ref_segment_type("ENV_VARIABLE").
cfg_ref_segment_type("ENV_WITH_DEFAULT").

// config_key_chain(Key, ReachedKey) — transitive placeholder reachability. Changing
// ReachedKey changes Key. Positive recursion; a cyclic config yields the cycle's
// keys, which is the honest answer.
config_key_chain(k, r) :- config_key_ref(k, r, _).
config_key_chain(k, r) :- config_key_ref(k, mid, _), config_key_chain(mid, r).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. NAME -> TYPE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// cfg_name_demand(Name): a string some knob says should name a class. Bounds every
// join below — without it the FQN lookup would run against every string in every
// config file.
// cfg_fqn_shaped(V): V LOOKS like a Java FQN — lowercase package segments, a final
// segment starting uppercase. Two jobs: it decides which arbitrary config strings
// are worth a type lookup at all, and it decides whether a FAILED lookup is worth
// reporting as an unknown (the value "8080" is not a broken class reference).
cfg_fqn_shaped(v) :- cfg_clean(_, v),
    match("[a-z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*(\\.[a-zA-Z0-9_]+)*\\.[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_$]*", v).

// (a) exact qualifiedName — the normal case for every config format.
cfg_name_type(n, "client", t) :- cfg_name_demand(n),
    java_type(_, n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
cfg_name_type(n, "lib", t) :- cfg_name_demand(n),
    lib_type(_, n, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// (b) NESTED type written with the binary `$` separator (com.x.Outer$Inner), which
// is what a config file must use for a nested class. The client IR names it by its
// dotted chain (com.x.Outer.Inner) and an older library IR by package + simple name;
// matching on the simple name after the last `$` serves both, at the cost of naming
// every same-named nested type when two exist.
cfg_dollar(n, i) :- cfg_name_demand(n), contains("$", n),
    i = range(0, strlen(n)), substr(n, i, 1) = "$".
cfg_dollar_last(n, m) :- cfg_dollar(n, _), m = max i : cfg_dollar(n, i).
cfg_nested_simple(n, s) :- cfg_dollar_last(n, m),
    s = substr(n, m + 1, strlen(n) - m - 1).
cfg_name_type(n, "client", t) :- cfg_nested_simple(n, s),
    java_type(s, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).
cfg_name_type(n, "lib", t) :- cfg_nested_simple(n, s),
    lib_type(s, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, t).

// (c) SIMPLE name resolved through IMPORTS. Only meaningful for an annotation
// argument, which lives in a .java file: `@JsonDeserialize(using = FooDeser.class)`
// records the argument value as the bare name `FooDeser`. A config FILE has no
// imports, so this clause is keyed on the annotation's file, never fired for
// XML/YAML/properties.
cfg_name_type(n, prov, t) :- cfg_ann_name_in_file(n, f),
    type_resolves_in_file(prov, n, f, t).

// cfg_name_resolved(Name) — guard for the unknown-reporting rules.
cfg_name_resolved(n) :- cfg_name_type(n, _, _).
